---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Chip from "../../components/atoms/Chip.astro";
---

<Layout title="RAG & AI Agent Service | Carlos Daniel">
	<main class="max-w-prose mx-auto px-6 lg:px-0 py-32">
		<!-- Back link -->
		<div class="mb-16 reveal">
			<a
				href="/"
				class="text-sm font-mono tracking-wide uppercase text-slate-500 hover:text-slate-900 transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 group"
			>
				<span class="transform group-hover:-translate-x-1 transition-transform"
					>←</span
				> Back to Index
			</a>
		</div>

		<!-- Meta Block -->
		<header class="mb-24 reveal">
			<h1 class="text-5xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-8 text-slate-900">
				End-to-End RAG & AI Agent Service
			</h1>

			<div
				class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-8 border-t border-b border-slate-200 py-8"
			>
				<div>
					<span
						class="block text-sm font-mono tracking-wide uppercase text-slate-400 mb-1"
						>Role</span
					>
					<span class="text-slate-900 font-medium">Lead Architect</span>
				</div>
				<div>
					<span
						class="block text-sm font-mono tracking-wide uppercase text-slate-400 mb-1"
						>Timeline</span
					>
					<span class="text-slate-900 font-medium">Q3 2024</span>
				</div>
				<div class="col-span-2 sm:col-span-2">
					<span
						class="block text-sm font-mono tracking-wide uppercase text-slate-400 mb-1"
						>Stack</span
					>
					<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
						<Chip>Python</Chip>
						<Chip>FastAPI</Chip>
						<Chip>Pinecone</Chip>
						<Chip>LangChain</Chip>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</header>

		<!-- The Article -->
		<article class="space-y-16 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600 reveal">
			<section>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-6 text-slate-900">
					The Constraint & Objective
				</h2>
				<p>
					Building a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline is
					straightforward; building one that scales elegantly under load while
					maintaining sub-200ms retrieval latency is not. The objective was to
					design an AI agent service capable of ingesting thousands of internal
					documents, indexing them with high-density embeddings, and answering
					complex user queries with strict source attribution.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-6 text-slate-900">
					System Architecture
				</h2>
				<div
					class="bg-slate-100 p-8 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto text-sm my-8 group"
				>
					<!-- Mermaid placeholder or diagram -->
					<div
						class="font-mono text-slate-500 mb-4 transition-opacity group-hover:text-slate-800"
					>
						[ User Query ] -> [ FastAPI Gateway ] -> [ Query Reformulation (LLM)
						] -> [ Vector Search (Pinecone) ] -> [ Context Assembly ] -> [ Final
						Generation (LLM) ]
					</div>
				</div>
				<p>
					The architecture heavily emphasizes decoupling the ingestion pipeline
					from the query pipeline. Ingestion runs asynchronously via message
					queues, chunking and embedding documents without blocking the main
					event loop.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-6 text-slate-900">
					Key Technical Decisions
				</h2>
				<ul class="space-y-4 list-disc pl-5">
					<li>
						<strong class="text-slate-900">Async Query Pipeline:</strong> Utilized
						Python's <code
							class="bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm font-mono"
							>asyncio</code
						> with FastAPI to ensure that long-running LLM network calls do not block
						the thread pool, drastically increasing throughput.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong class="text-slate-900">Hybrid Search:</strong> Implemented a combination
						of dense vector embeddings and sparse keyword search (BM25) to improve
						retrieval accuracy on domain-specific terminology.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong class="text-slate-900">Streaming Responses:</strong> Implemented
						Server-Sent Events (SSE) to stream tokens back to the client immediately,
						reducing perceived latency to near-zero.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-6 text-slate-900">
					The Implementation
				</h2>
				<p class="mb-4">
					A crucial part of the performance optimization was managing concurrent
					LLM calls for query reformulation. Here is a simplified snippet of the
					async gathering logic:
				</p>
				<pre
					class="bg-[#0a0a0a] text-slate-200 p-6 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm shadow-xl"><code>async def execute_rag_pipeline(query: str):
    # 1. Reformulate query
    optimized_queries = await llm.generate_variations(query)
    
    # 2. Concurrent vector searches
    search_tasks = [
        vector_db.search_async(q, top_k=3) 
        for q in optimized_queries
    ]
    results = await asyncio.gather(*search_tasks)
    
    # 3. Deduplicate and rank
    context = rank_and_filter(results)
    
    # 4. Stream generation
    return StreamingResponse(
        llm.stream_answer(query, context), 
        media_type="text/event-stream"
    )</code></pre>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-6 text-slate-900">
					Outcomes
				</h2>
				<p>
					The system successfully reduced P95 retrieval latency by 40% compared
					to the naive implementation, while the hybrid search approach
					increased the MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank) of the retrieved contexts by
					22%.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.reveal {
		opacity: 0;
		transform: translateY(16px);
		transition:
			opacity 0.6s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
			transform 0.6s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
	}

	.reveal.is-visible {
		opacity: 1;
		transform: translateY(0);
	}
</style>

<script>
	const setupReveals = () => {
		const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
			(entries) => {
				entries.forEach((entry) => {
					if (entry.isIntersecting) {
						entry.target.classList.add("is-visible");
						observer.unobserve(entry.target);
					}
				});
			},
			{ rootMargin: "0px", threshold: 0.1 },
		);

		document
			.querySelectorAll(".reveal")
			.forEach((elem) => observer.observe(elem));
	};
	setupReveals();
	document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", setupReveals);
</script>
